    CI: stop stalled apt steps from hanging jobs for hours (#171)
    
    A run had three of four data-generation shards sit in "Install OSMesa
    dependencies" for 2h26m before being cancelled. apt has no timeout of its
    own, the step had none, and the job had none, so a stalled mirror could hold
    a runner until GitHub's 6 hour default.
    
    - Cap each apt attempt at 100s and retry 3 times, so a stall becomes a retry
      rather than a hang -- a plain retry loop never fires on a stall, because the
      command never returns. Add --fix-missing, as the CUDA step already does.
    - Cap those steps at 5 minutes, enforced by the runner, so the worst case does
      not depend on the shell logic being right.
    - Give unit-tests, data-generation-tests and ruff-checks a 60 minute job
      timeout as a runaway backstop. Measured durations on a good run were 6m,
      12m49s and 21s; curobo keeps its existing 90 (measured 23m19s).
    - Drop the CUDA retry backoff from 15s to 3s.
    - Detach the disk cleanup: it cost ~85s of every job and nothing waited for
      the space. /opt/hostedtoolcache is no longer deleted, since later steps can
      read from it and a background delete would race them.
    
    Two deliberate visibility tradeoffs from detaching the cleanup: `df -h /` now
    reports space before the delete finishes, and the delete's output is
    discarded. Both are noted inline.
    
    Recovery is not guaranteed -- killing apt mid-transaction can leave a dpkg
    lock, so a retry may burn an attempt and the step still fail. Failing in 5
    minutes instead of 2.5 hours is the point.
    
